WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.020
How do rolling hashes make

2
00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:04.400
Rabin–Karp work? We will encode

3
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.700
a window, derive its constant-work

4
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.680
rolling update, and follow a

5
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.240
collision all the way through

6
00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.000
actual character verification.

7
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.100
Map alphabet characters to integer

8
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:16.560
codes and interpret a window

9
00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.280
of m characters as a

10
00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.740
base-d number. The leftmost code

11
00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:22.340
multiplies d to the m

12
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.720
minus one, with successive codes

13
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.300
using decreasing powers.

14
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.060
Horner evaluation builds this number

15
00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:30.680
by multiplying the current value

16
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.180
by d and adding the

17
00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.420
next code. This requires one

18
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.280
pass through m symbols instead

19
00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:37.960
of separately computing a power

20
00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:38.740
for every term.

21
00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.092
For decimal window three six

22
00:00:41.092 --> 00:00:42.760
four one five followed by

23
00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:44.700
an incoming two, remove the

24
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.160
leading three times ten thousand.

25
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.920
Multiply the remainder by ten

26
00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.244
and append two, obtaining six

27
00:00:51.244 --> 00:00:52.780
four one five two.

28
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.080
The general update subtracts the

29
00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.060
outgoing code times d to

30
00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.880
the m minus one, then

31
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.600
multiplies by d and adds

32
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.300
the incoming code. Precompute that

33
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.440
leading place value, since it

34
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:06.580
is the same at every

35
00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:07.040
alignment.

36
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.200
Exact encodings become enormous for

37
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.620
long patterns, so Rabin–Karp keeps

38
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:14.480
their residues modulo a chosen

39
00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.300
prime q. Compute both initial

40
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.580
hashes with modular Horner evaluation

41
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.540
and precompute H modulo q.

42
00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.580
Now take decimal pattern twenty-five,

43
00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.740
text one two two five,

44
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.740
base ten and modulus thirteen.

45
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.120
The pattern hash is twelve,

46
00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:32.600
and the first text window

47
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.200
twelve also hashes to twelve,

48
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:35.520
even though the strings differ.

49
00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.400
Equal hashes therefore request a

50
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.100
character check rather than proving

51
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.400
success. Comparing the first digits

52
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.700
rejects window twelve immediately, because

53
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.440
pattern two differs from text

54
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:46.780
one.

55
00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.080
Roll to window twenty-two, whose

56
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:51.720
hash is nine, so its

57
00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.500
different hash rejects it without

58
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:56.040
character verification. Roll once more

59
00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.080
to window twenty-five, whose hash

60
00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.640
is twelve and whose two

61
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.200
characters both match, giving zero-based

62
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.280
index two.

63
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.020
With H equal to ten,

64
00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:06.740
the first modular roll is

65
00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.100
twelve minus one times ten,

66
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.180
multiplied by ten, plus two,

67
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.800
all modulo thirteen. The result

68
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.160
is twenty-two modulo thirteen, or

69
00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.060
nine; the next roll must

70
00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.360
normalise its negative intermediate residue

71
00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.120
if the language uses signed

72
00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:22.780
remainders.

73
00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.340
Initial hashing costs big O

74
00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.100
of m, and each rolling

75
00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.260
update uses constant word arithmetic.

76
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.160
Verification adds work whenever hashes

77
00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:33.940
agree, so a full runtime

78
00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:35.800
statement must count those candidate

79
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.740
checks rather than declaring every

80
00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.320
scan unconditionally linear.

81
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.260
Things to take note of:

82
00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:42.880
use the same encoding and

83
00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.100
modulus for both hashes, precompute

84
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:46.780
the leading place value, and

85
00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:49.440
verify every equal-hash candidate. Roll

86
00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.060
only when another complete window

87
00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.740
exists, preserve modulo conventions, and

88
00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:54.920
remember that a collision is

89
00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:56.480
expected behaviour for a hash,

90
00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.200
not permission to return the

91
00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:58.760
wrong answer.
